### How do you define a TriFunction?

Declare a @FunctionalInterface with three type parameters and one abstract method.

```java
package com.bank.core;

@FunctionalInterface
public interface TriFunction<A, B, C, R> {
    R apply(A a, B b, C c);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;

public class TriFunctionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simple interest: principal * rate * years / 100
        TriFunction<BigDecimal, BigDecimal, Integer, BigDecimal>
            simpleInterest = (principal, rate, years) ->
                principal.multiply(rate)
                    .multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(years))
                    .divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(100), 2,
                        RoundingMode.HALF_UP);

        BigDecimal interest = simpleInterest.apply(
            new BigDecimal("100000"),
            new BigDecimal("8.5"),
            3);

        System.out.println("Interest: " + interest);
    }
}
```

```text
Interest: 25500.00
```

### Why does Java have no built-in TriFunction?

For each arity, the library would also need primitive specialisations (IntTriFunction, ToDoubleTriFunction, etc.), multiplying the number of interfaces. The designers drew the line at two arguments, which covers the overwhelming majority of real use cases.

| Arity | Interfaces needed (approx) |
|---|---|
| 1 arg | Function, IntFunction, ToIntFunction, ... (40+) |
| 2 args | BiFunction, ToDoubleBiFunction, ... (20+) |
| 3 args | Would need 30+ more interfaces |

### When should you use a parameter object instead?

A parameter object bundles multiple inputs into a named class, which scales better and is more evolvable.

```java
// Parameter object
record LoanParams(BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal rate, int years) {}

Function<LoanParams, BigDecimal> interestCalc = p ->
    p.principal().multiply(p.rate())
        .multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(p.years()))
        .divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(100), 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);

BigDecimal result = interestCalc.apply(
    new LoanParams(new BigDecimal("100000"),
        new BigDecimal("8.5"), 3));
```

| Approach | Scales to more inputs? | Named inputs? | Evolvable? |
|---|---|---|---|
| TriFunction | No (4th arg needs QuadFunction) | No | No |
| Parameter object | Yes | Yes | Yes (add fields) |

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: not adding @FunctionalInterface.

```java
// Without the annotation, someone could add a second abstract method later
// breaking all lambdas. Always add @FunctionalInterface for safety.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- A TriFunction is fine for a quick, localised need. For an evolving API, prefer a parameter object.
- Currying is another alternative: convert TriFunction into nested Functions: a -> b -> c -> result.
- Some libraries (Vavr, Apache Commons) provide TriFunction and beyond.
- The trade-off is brevity (TriFunction) vs growth (parameter object).

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Can you curry a TriFunction?" Yes: convert to Function<A, Function<B, Function<C, R>>>.
- "Does Vavr library provide TriFunction?" Yes, up to Function8.
- "How do you add andThen to TriFunction?" Add a default method: default <V> TriFunction<A, B, C, V> andThen(Function<R, V> after).
